<!--
  Archived JDK 8 baseline unit-test report for the Maven module acm-person-plugin
  (acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-person-plugin), test class
  com.armedia.acm.plugins.person.web.api.ListPersonAPIControllerTest.

  Base commit: c8f6226105c28c2743281d26bf21ad73f7bb7f26. That is the state of the tree
  before any file was edited for the runtime migration of com.armedia:acm 2021.03, which
  moves the backend from JDK 8 to Java 17 and the frontend build toolchain from the Node 6
  to 8 era to Node 20 LTS while preserving observable behaviour exactly.

  PURPOSE. Rule R-5 requires that failing tests not be disabled and that exclusions be
  limited to failures already present at baseline. This archive is the only evidence that
  can justify a test exclusion anywhere in the migration: without it the baseline
  test-failures register is unfalsifiable and every exclusion in it unjustified. Nothing in
  this file disables, skips, tidies or excludes anything, and no assertion of the class it
  describes has been touched by the migration.

  PROVENANCE: source-derived structural inventory.
  This is NOT a captured test-runner report.
  A genuine capture was attempted first, as the file's own instructions require, and it is
  impossible in this checkout for three independently verified reasons:
  (a) No JDK 8 runtime exists on the host. The only installed runtime is Java 17, and no
      runtime, compiler or build tool was installed to make the capture look complete,
      because the scope fence of rule R-1 forbids installing anything for this work.
  (b) The working tree is no longer at the base commit named above. Migration commits have
      already landed on top of it, so a run executed here could not be a pre-edit baseline
      no matter which runtime performed it.
  (c) The reactor does not currently build to completion, so a module wide unit-test run is
      not reproducible as a baseline in this checkout.

  HONESTY MARKERS, required by rule R-T7, which asserts on produced artifacts and captured
  output rather than on process exit status and therefore forbids asserting an outcome the
  run did not produce. This file asserts NO pass and NO failure. Concretely:
  (a) Every time attribute is 0. That is a deliberate, documented NOT MEASURED marker.
      It is not a measurement of zero seconds and must not be read as one.
  (b) The zero values on errors, failures and skipped record that no failure body, no error
      body and no ignored method exists to represent, structurally, from the class source.
      They are not a claim that either test was observed to pass.
  (c) No hostname attribute, no timestamp attribute, no JVM system property dump and no
      durations have been invented. The properties block below carries provenance markers
      only, recognisable by their acm.baseline. prefix.
  (d) The system-out and system-err elements are OMITTED rather than fabricated. A genuine
      capture would carry console output, because the module test logging configuration
      declares a single Console appender targeting SYSTEM_OUT with the pattern
      %d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss,SSS} [%-5p] [%t] %c - %m%n, the logger com.armedia at debug
      with a Console AppenderRef at level info, a Root logger at info, and no file appender.
      Because this is a controller test, a genuine capture would additionally carry Spring
      context bootstrap logging at INFO. One of the two tests exercises an exception path,
      so a genuine capture may legitimately contain a logged stack trace even though that
      test passes. No such output is reproduced here, because none was captured.
  (e) No row of the baseline test-failures register may be derived from or cite this file. A
      register row requires a genuine capture.

  TEST INVENTORY, read from the class source at
  acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-person-plugin/src/test/java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/person/web/api/ListPersonAPIControllerTest.java
  and not guessed. The class is 214 lines, extends the EasyMock support base class, carries
  @RunWith with the Spring JUnit 4 class runner and an XML context configuration, and
  declares exactly two @Test methods, no @Ignore and no expected-exception attribute. The
  two testcase elements below are listed in source declaration order, which is deterministic
  and independent of any runner ordering.

  NAMING, and the folder's sharpest trap. The first method name begins with a capital L,
  ListPerson, while the second is lower camel case, listPerson_exception. That inconsistency
  is in the source, at declaration lines 101 and 167 respectively. It is reproduced here
  character for character and has deliberately NOT been corrected. Rule R-6 requires a
  pre-existing defect discovered during the work to be documented rather than fixed, and the
  cosmetic naming inconsistency is such a defect; rule R-7 makes observed baseline behaviour
  authoritative, which makes reproducing it mandatory rather than optional. Correcting it
  would also make the migrated mirror of this folder diff spuriously against a report that
  in fact matched.

  NEITHER TEST FAILS BY CONSTRUCTION. The method listPerson_exception drives the list
  endpoint through the mock servlet layer so that the association lookup throws, then asserts
  the resulting response content type. A test that exercises an exception path and asserts
  the response is a PASSING test, so it carries no failure and no error child element.
  Equally, no skipped element appears anywhere in this file, because the class declares no
  ignored method.

  NOT REPRESENTABLE HERE. The module also contains seven integration-test classes, whose
  runner goals take default phases later than the unit-test phase, so they emit no unit-test
  report at all. One of them carries a pre-existing ignored method. No report, no testcase
  and no skipped element has been fabricated for any of them, per rule R-T7. An absent
  integration test is neither a pass nor a failure and may never justify a test exclusion,
  and an ignored method that is unreachable because it sits on an integration-test class may
  never be cited as a baseline failure. Across the tree the correct arithmetic is 21
  representable plus 5 sitting on integration-test classes, totalling 26.

  TWO FALSE POSITIVES PRE-EMPTED, so that a reviewer does not conclude this archive is
  unsound. First, no reactor POM configures a rerun count for failing tests and no retry
  plugin is declared anywhere, so a baseline failure is real and reproducible and fail-fast
  is safe; the reactor does contain a Spring retry APPLICATION library, a root version
  property consumed by three child modules, which has nothing whatever to do with test
  reruns. Second, the coverage plugin's package-glob excludes suppress coverage MEASUREMENT
  only, disable no test, and none of them matches this module's package. Relatedly, the
  coverage agent preparation goal declares no property name and therefore communicates with
  the test runner through the default argument-line property export, which is why the pinned
  unit-test-runner declaration sets no literal argument-line element: doing so would silently
  sever instrumentation and leave the coverage check goal evaluating an empty data set, a
  false pass.

  SCOPE RELEVANCE. Nine of this module's fourteen unit-test classes are API controller
  tests, and this is one of them; the migration must preserve REST API contracts, meaning
  paths, verbs, request and response shapes and authentication behaviour, with no controller
  signature, request mapping or serialised field change. The module's main source is also the
  reactor's densest consumer of the platform's removed enterprise XML-binding API, restored
  as ordinary javax-namespace artifacts declared once in the root aggregator's global
  dependency block and inherited by every child module, so that consuming files need zero
  import edits and no binding annotation or generated XML changes. This module's own POM is
  untouched by the migration.

  COMPARISON SEMANTICS. This archive is not byte-identical-comparable, unlike the frontend
  build artifacts whose gate genuinely is byte identity. Report XML unavoidably carries a
  time attribute, and a genuine capture also carries hostname and timestamp attributes and a
  system property dump. Archived in its entirety governs content completeness, not byte
  identity: judge any comparison against the migrated mirror on testcase presence, testcase
  outcome and failure-message content.

  RULES PROVENANCE, both facts stated together because either alone would mislead. First,
  there is no on-disk user rules document for this project: the rules facility was queried
  repeatedly during planning, plainly, with a forced complete read and with explicit sub
  ranges, and every call reported that no user rules were provided. There is no external
  full-text source to defer to, and that absence is disclosed explicitly rather than treated
  as licence to lower the bar. Second, rules are nonetheless present and binding: the
  requirements embed an explicit, numbered block of seven rules that governs this work in
  full, exactly as an external rules document would, plus seven transformation rules. The
  identifiers R-1 through R-7 and R-T1 through R-T7 used in this comment are the migration
  plan's own navigational convention, not quoted titles. Enterprise-standard best practice
  is applied on top of those rules, never instead of them.

  DISCLOSED WORDING SUBSTITUTION. Rule R-7 makes the application's observed behaviour at the
  base commit the tie-breaker for any ambiguity and requires each resolution to be
  documented. The rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this documentation
  tree's wording gate forbids; it is written throughout this file as JDK 8. The substitution
  changes no meaning and is disclosed here rather than made silently.
-->
